# Source pins for the runtime dependencies of this repository's Python scripts.
# This file lists only the top-level packages; the fully resolved, hash-pinned
# lock that pip actually installs is requirements.txt (generated from this file).
#
# Every package is pinned to an exact version from PyPI, a trusted language
# package registry. The generated lock additionally pins the full transitive
# closure to exact versions and SHA-256 hashes so pip --require-hashes can
# refuse any artifact whose hash does not match.
#
# PyYAML parses YAML: workflows for privileged-checkout-guard and for
# shared/workflow_inputs.py (which the label actions' command-line tools use),
# and workflows and action.yml files for the tests. The scripts' GitHub API
# calls use the standard library.
#
# Regenerating the lock (do this whenever a pin here changes):
#
#   uv pip compile --universal --python-version 3.11 --generate-hashes \
#       requirements.in -o requirements.txt
#
# That command keeps the versions already present in the lock, so on its own it
# only re-resolves what a changed pin in this file forces. Transitive releases
# published since the last run are left alone, including security fixes for
# packages nothing here pins directly. Add --upgrade to re-resolve the whole
# closure, or -P/--upgrade-package NAME to move one package and whatever it
# drags with it, which keeps a security bump to a reviewable diff.
#
# --universal + --python-version 3.11 make the lock a superset that covers every
# interpreter the actions run under, so any marker-gated entry resolves
# correctly wherever pip runs it under --require-hashes.
#
# The floor is 3.11, so the lock does not cover Python 3.10.
# Raise the floor again, and regenerate every lock, when packages start dropping
# 3.11; check first that no action still runs the version being dropped.
PyYAML==6.0.3
